Deep-level features:
- Click any stage to see its configuration and execution history.
- Identify the first failed stage.
- Display dependent jobs and feeds.
- Show the reason for failure, where evidence is available.
- Identify downstream processes affected by the failure.
- Compare current execution against the last successful run.
The dependency map should be configurable for each application instead of being hardcoded.
2. Advanced feed monitoring
Your NAS module can do much more than check whether a file exists.
Feature	What it does
Expected arrival monitoring	Checks whether a file arrived before its deadline
File freshness	Detects files that haven't been updated
File size comparison	Flags unexpected changes in file size
Record-count validation	Compares actual and expected record counts
Duplicate detection	Identifies files that may have been delivered more than once
Zero-byte detection	Identifies empty files
File integrity	Validates checksums when available
Processing confirmation	Verifies that the application processed the file
Historical comparison	Compares current delivery with previous successful runs
Late-feed prediction	Estimates whether a feed may miss its deadline based on historical data


Example: Feed details
When an engineer clicks a red feed, display:
- Feed name and NAS path
- Expected arrival time
- Actual arrival time
- Delay duration
- Last successful delivery
- Previous file size and record count
- Current processing status
- Dependent AutoSys and TWS jobs
- Related application errors
- Investigation history
Important: File arrival and successful processing must be treated as separate states. A file can exist in the NAS directory while its processing job has failed.
3. AutoSys and TWS job intelligence
Create a dedicated job-monitoring screen with separate tabs for AutoSys and TWS.
Advanced job features
